Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT vs YAML — Values, free text and validation
	Metadata:
		Author: Claude Fable 5
		Revision: Joan Costa Mombiela
		Last modif: 2026-09-08
		Description: STXT and YAML compared with examples: implicit typing versus text values, block scalars versus literal blocks, attack surface, and validation inside the language.

	Header: @STXT@ vs YAML

	Assert >>
		Both languages are built on indentation, but they optimize for different things:\\
		YAML for serializing data structures, @STXT@ for documents written and read by people.

	Content >>
		YAML and @STXT@ both express hierarchy with indentation, and a simple document looks
		alike in the two. The differences are underneath: what type a value has, how many
		spellings the same structure admits, how multi-line text is written, and what the
		language itself checks.

		There is also a difference of model: YAML has no namespaces and no schemas.
		Nothing in the file declares which vocabulary it belongs to or which structure it
		should be validated against. In @STXT@ the namespace is part of the language
		([STXT-SPEC §7](stxt-core-ref#s7)) and connects each document with its definition.

	Subheader: The same content, twice

	Content >>
		A service descriptor in YAML:

	Listing >>
		service: billing
		replicas: 2
		ports:
		  - 8080
		  - 8443
		maintainer: ana@example.com
		notes: |
		  Restarted after the 2026-08-31 incident.
		  The TLS certificate expires on 2027-03-01.

	Content >>
		And in @STXT@:

	Code >>
		Service:
			Name: billing
			Replicas: 2
			Ports:
				Port: 8080
				Port: 8443
			Maintainer: ana@example.com
			Notes >>
				Restarted after the 2026-08-31 incident.
				The TLS certificate expires on 2027-03-01.

	Content >>
		Three differences already visible in this example:

		* **Values.** In YAML `replicas: 2` is an integer and `replicas: "2"` a string:
		  the spelling decides the type. In @STXT@ every value is text; the type, when
		  needed, is declared by the definition and checked by the validator, not the
		  parser.
		* **Lists.** YAML has a block syntax for sequences (`- 8080`) and a flow syntax
		  (`[8080, 8443]`), and both coexist in the same document. @STXT@ has no list
		  syntax: a repeated child is the list, with a container node such as `Ports` or
		  without one ([FAQ](faq#why-are-there-no-lists)).
		* **Indentation.** YAML accepts any number of spaces per level, fixed per document,
		  and forbids tabs. @STXT@ fixes one tab or four spaces per level, and mixing them
		  on the same line is a parse error.

	Subheader: When the spelling changes the meaning

	Content >>
		In YAML an unquoted scalar resolves to a type according to its form, and the
		resolution rules changed between versions 1.1 and 1.2 of the language:

	Listing >>
		country: NO         # false in YAML 1.1; the string "NO" in 1.2
		version: 3.10       # the number 3.1 in both
		window: 12:30       # the integer 750 in YAML 1.1 (sexagesimal); a string in 1.2
		enabled: yes        # true in 1.1; the string "yes" in 1.2

	Content >>
		Keeping `NO`, `3.10` or `12:30` as text requires quoting them, and knowing when
		requires knowing the resolution rules of the version the parser implements. In
		@STXT@ the same document has no such ambiguity: a value is always the characters
		written.

	Code >>
		Settings:
			Country: NO
			Version: 3.10
			Window: 12:30
			Enabled: yes

	Content >>
		If `Enabled` must be a boolean, the definition says so (`Enabled: (1) BOOLEAN`,
		which accepts `true` and `false`) and the validator rejects `yes` with an error
		code instead of converting it.

	Subheader: Multi-line text

	Content >>
		A YAML block scalar combines three indicators: the style (`|` literal or `>`
		folded), the handling of the final line break (`-`, `+` or none) and, when needed,
		the indentation. Each combination produces a different string:

	Listing >>
		literal: |
		  first line
		  second line
		folded: >
		  first line
		  second line
		kept: |+
		  first line

		next: value

	Content >>
		`literal` is `"first line\nsecond line\n"`; `folded` is `"first line second line\n"`;
		`kept` also keeps the empty line that follows it. @STXT@ has a single construct:
		everything indented under a `>>` node is text as written, with no variants, no
		escapes and no interpretation. Trailing whitespace on each line and trailing empty
		lines are discarded; additional indentation and intermediate empty lines are kept
		([STXT-SPEC §10](stxt-core-ref#s10)).

	Code >>
		Notes >>
			first line
			second line

	Subheader: Parsing and the attack surface

	Content >>
		YAML includes anchors and aliases (`&a`, `*a`) and type tags (`!!str`,
		`!!python/object`, `!ruby/object`). Aliases let a small document expand into a
		huge structure in memory, and implementation-specific tags have allowed arbitrary
		objects to be instantiated while loading a document; hence the `safe_load`
		functions of the libraries. @STXT@ has no references, anchors, tags, inclusion or
		entities, and parsing is linear, line by line, in a single pass. The threat model
		is normative: [STXT-SPEC §15](stxt-core-ref#s15).

	Subheader: Validation

	Content >>
		YAML has no schema layer: it is validated with external tools, usually JSON Schema
		applied to the loaded structure. In @STXT@ the schema layer is part of the
		language, optional, with a closed content model. The descriptor above, with a
		namespace and without the `Ports` container: the `(+)` cardinality in the template
		already says that `Port` repeats.

	Code >>
		Service (com.example.deploy):
			Name: billing
			Replicas: 2
			Port: 8080
			Port: 8443
			Maintainer: ana@example.com
			Notes >>
				The TLS certificate expires on 2027-03-01.

	Content >>
		And the template for its namespace:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.deploy
			Structure >>
				Service (com.example.deploy):
					Name: (1)
					Replicas: (1) NATURAL
					Port: (+) NATURAL
					Maintainer: (1) EMAIL
					Notes: (?) TEXT

	Content >>
		An undeclared name, a missing `Port` or `Replicas: two` fail with an explicit
		error code:

	Code >>
		# ERROR: Replicas requires a NATURAL value
		Service (com.example.deploy):
			Name: billing
			Replicas: two
			Port: 8080
			Maintainer: ana@example.com

	Subheader: What about NestedText?

	Content >>
		NestedText makes several of the same decisions as @STXT@: every value is text, no
		implicit typing, no escapes, and structure lives in the indentation. It keeps the
		YAML data model —dictionaries, lists and strings— and leaves types and validation
		to the application; it has no namespaces and no schemas. For transporting data
		with a predictable syntax, both arrive at the same place; @STXT@ adds the named
		vocabulary and validation inside the language.

	Subheader: When to use each

	Content >>
		YAML, where the ecosystem expects it: Kubernetes manifests, CI pipelines, Docker
		Compose, Ansible.

		@STXT@, when the file is a document that people write and read: configuration
		with notes in prose, [corporate documents](use-cases-corporate-docs),
		[CMS content](use-cases-cms), and any structure that must be validated without
		external tools. The [configuration files](use-cases-config-files) use case
		develops the closest scenario, and the short version of this comparison is in
		the [FAQ](faq#why-not-xml-yaml-or-toml).
